{
  "concept": "Alanine’s titration curve links added base to pH, dominant protonation state, and net charge, with pKa points marking 50:50 mixtures and the isoelectric point marking the neutral zwitterion.",
  "figureType": "line_plot",
  "elements": [
    "pale blue plot background",
    "vertical pH axis labeled 'pH' ranging from 0 to 12",
    "horizontal axis labeled 'Equivalents of HO−' ranging from 0.0 to 2.0",
    "grid lines aligned with pH ticks every 2 units and equivalents ticks every 0.5",
    "magenta alanine titration curve with two buffering regions and two steep rises",
    "movable marker on the titration curve",
    "dynamic pH readout near the marker",
    "x marker at pKa1 at 0.5 equivalents and pH 2.34",
    "annotation label 'pKa1 = 2.34' with arrow to the first pKa point",
    "x marker at the isoelectric point at 1.0 equivalent and pH 6.01",
    "annotation label 'Isoelectric point = 6.01' with arrow to the isoelectric point",
    "x marker at pKa2 at 1.5 equivalents and pH 9.69",
    "annotation label 'pKa2 = 9.69' with arrow to the second pKa point",
    "thin leader arrow from the low-pH start of the curve to fully protonated alanine",
    "thin leader arrow from the pKa1 region to a bracketed 50:50 pair of fully protonated alanine and zwitterionic alanine",
    "thin leader arrow from the isoelectric point to zwitterionic alanine",
    "thin leader arrow from the pKa2 region to a bracketed 50:50 pair of zwitterionic alanine and deprotonated alanine",
    "thin leader arrow from the high-pH end of the curve to fully deprotonated alanine",
    "fully protonated alanine structure H3N+CH(CH3)COOH with ammonium nitrogen and acidic OH highlighted magenta",
    "bracketed pKa1 species pair: H3N+CH(CH3)COOH plus H3N+CH(CH3)COO−",
    "zwitterionic alanine structure H3N+CH(CH3)COO−",
    "bracketed pKa2 species pair: H3N+CH(CH3)COO− plus H2NCH(CH3)COO−",
    "fully deprotonated alanine structure H2NCH(CH3)COO− with neutral amine nitrogen highlighted blue",
    "plus signs between paired structures at pKa1 and pKa2",
    "square brackets around paired-equilibrium structures",
    "species-view molecular formula labels",
    "net-charge-view charge labels showing +1, 0, and −1 states",
    "highlight halo for the selected landmark point and its linked structural formula or structural pair"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "baseEquivalents",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Equivalents of HO−",
      "range": [
        0,
        2,
        0.01
      ],
      "default": 1,
      "teaches": "Moving along the titration curve shows how adding base raises pH and shifts alanine from the fully protonated cation, through the zwitterion, to the fully deprotonated anion"
    },
    {
      "id": "rightPanelView",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Right panel view",
      "options": [
        "Species",
        "Net charge"
      ],
      "default": "Species",
      "teaches": "The molecular structures and the charge summary are two views of the same acid-base changes, making the zero net charge of the zwitterion at the isoelectric point explicit"
    },
    {
      "id": "landmarkSelect",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "Clicking pKa1, the isoelectric point, or pKa2 distinguishes 50:50 mixture points from the single dominant zwitterionic form at the isoelectric point"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Start fully protonated",
      "narration": "At very low equivalents of added base, alanine is mostly fully protonated: the amino group is H3N+ and the carboxyl group is COOH, giving a net positive charge.",
      "state": {
        "baseEquivalents": 0,
        "rightPanelView": "Species"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "First buffering point",
      "narration": "At 0.5 equivalents, the curve passes through pKa1 = 2.34. This is a 50:50 mixture of fully protonated alanine and the zwitterion, because the carboxyl proton is half removed.",
      "state": {
        "baseEquivalents": 0.5,
        "rightPanelView": "Species"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Isoelectric zwitterion",
      "narration": "At 1.0 equivalent, alanine is mostly the zwitterion H3N+CH(CH3)COO−. The positive ammonium and negative carboxylate charges cancel, so the net charge is zero.",
      "state": {
        "baseEquivalents": 1,
        "rightPanelView": "Net charge"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Second buffering point",
      "narration": "At 1.5 equivalents, the curve reaches pKa2 = 9.69. The zwitterion and fully deprotonated anion are present in equal amounts as the ammonium proton is half removed.",
      "state": {
        "baseEquivalents": 1.5,
        "rightPanelView": "Species"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "End fully deprotonated",
      "narration": "Near 2.0 equivalents, alanine is mostly H2NCH(CH3)COO−. Both acidic protons have been removed, leaving the molecule with a net negative charge.",
      "state": {
        "baseEquivalents": 2,
        "rightPanelView": "Net charge"
      }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "The pKa points are half-conversion mixtures, while the isoelectric point is where the dominant alanine form has equal positive and negative internal charges and therefore zero net charge.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Recreate the figure as a wide layout with the titration plot on the left and alanine structures on the right connected by thin arrows. Use a pale blue gridded plotting area, black axes, and a bright magenta titration curve. The curve should rise gently near pH 2.34, steeply near one equivalent, flatten through the upper buffer region near pH 9.69, then rise again toward pH 12. Mark pKa1, the isoelectric point, and pKa2 with small x marks and leader annotations. The right panel should stack structures vertically from fully protonated at the bottom to fully deprotonated at the top; show bracketed pairs at the two pKa levels and single dominant species at the extremes and isoelectric point. Color protonated amino nitrogens magenta and neutral amino nitrogens blue. The slider marker should snap visually to the curve and update the highlighted arrow and structure region as it moves."
}

